            <ArticleTitle>Case report of an anaphylactoid reaction to arginine.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>67-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Arginine is an agent commonly used to evaluate adequacy of growth hormone (GH) secretion. Because arginine is a simple amino acid, it is considered safe and rarely causes adverse reactions.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To report the second anaphylactoid reaction to arginine in a child undergoing stimulation testing with arginine for assessing GH secretion.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Allergy skin testing to arginine was performed with a protocol similar to penicillin testing 4 weeks after the anaphylactoid reaction.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Testing revealed a positive response to the arginine.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The use of intravenous arginine as a test of GH reserve remains safe and effective, but it is prudent to have the equipment and medication available to treat an allergic reaction.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
